Chapter: SectorReadListGrowth
  cites Foreword chapter List

 The mechanism is worth stating, because nothing about the symptom pointed at
 it. `block-read-sector` allocates its 512-byte buffer at the top of the bump
 heap and asks the disk to fill it, and the fill is `rep insw`, which codex-vm
 services by storing into guest RAM from the HOST side. Guest RAM is reserved
 and committed lazily on a guest fault, and a store the guest never performs
 raises no such fault, so the destination stayed reserved and the host process
 died on it: a Windows access violation with no diagnostic from the emulator
 and no chance for the guest to write one either. Not an `!EXC`, and not the
 heap running into the stack.

 That is why it takes both halves to see it, and why each half alone looks
 innocent. Six thousand sector reads with no list finish in seconds, because a
 function returning resets the bump heap and every buffer lands back in the
 same already-committed page. A list grown to well over a million elements
 with no disk finishes too, because the guest touches every page it writes.
 Only a read whose result is RETAINED marches the heap top into fresh pages,
 and then the next buffer is allocated somewhere the guest has not been.

Section: Entry

  copy-bytes : Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  copy-bytes (buf) (off) (count) (acc) =
    if count <= 0 then acc
    else copy-bytes buf (off + 1) (count - 1) (list-push acc (peek-byte buf off))

  loop-sectors : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> [Device.Block] List Integer
  loop-sectors (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else act
      buf <- block-read-sector i
      loop-sectors (i + 1) n (copy-bytes buf 0 512 acc)
    end

  opening : [Console, Device.Block] Nothing
  opening = act
    print-line-uni "start"
    xs <- loop-sectors 0 3000 []
    print-line-uni ("len " & show (list-length xs))
  end
